{
  "id": "PSI-159",
  "summary_plain": "This investigation burned simple hydrocarbon gases like propane, n-butane, and ethane on spherical porous burners inside the International Space Station to study cool diffusion flames. Understanding cool flames helps researchers improve the efficiency of internal combustion engines on Earth. It also provides fundamental data to advance spacecraft fire prevention and combustion safety.",
  "summary_expert": "Spherical diffusion flame tests were performed aboard the International Space Station under microgravity conditions using gaseous fuels including ethane, propane, and n-butane. The testing varied reactant dilutions, flow rates, chamber pressures (including tests at 0.5 atm, 1 atm, and 3 atm), normal convection directions, and inverse convection modes where oxygen/nitrogen mixtures flowed outward into surrounding fuel-nitrogen mixtures. Observations were captured using cameras, radiometers, photomultiplier tubes, and thermocouples to evaluate cool diffusion flame kinetics, stabilization limits, and oscillatory extinction phenomena.",
  "key_findings": [
    {
      "text": "A total of 280 hot flames were ignited or naturally reignited during Increments 64 through 66 for the CFI-G experiment, with 15 cool flames subsequently identified.",
      "source": {
        "psi_id": "PSI-159",
        "doi": "10.60555/cyt1-kh64",
        "file": "documents/PSI-159/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf",
        "page": 38
      },
      "provenance": "Google Gemini (free tier)"
    },
    {
      "text": "CFI-G testing comprised two rounds, where the first round featured normal flames with fuel flowing into an oxygen/nitrogen atmosphere, and the second round largely used inverse-flame tests with an oxygen/nitrogen mixture flowing into a surrounding nitrogen and gaseous fuel mixture.",
      "source": {
        "psi_id": "PSI-159",
        "doi": "10.60555/cyt1-kh64",
        "file": "documents/PSI-159/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf",
        "page": 38
      },
      "provenance": "Google Gemini (free tier)"
    },
    {
      "text": "The exposed type-B thermocouple containing platinum acted as a catalyst for hydrocarbon oxidation, contributing to rapid flame reignition and limiting low-temperature combustion durations during certain tests.",
      "source": {
        "psi_id": "PSI-159",
        "doi": "10.60555/cyt1-kh64",
        "file": "documents/PSI-159/PSI-159_Reports_FinalRpt_ACME.CFI-G.pdf",
        "page": 15
      },
      "provenance": "Google Gemini (free tier)"
    }
